1. MTU (Maximum Transmission Unit)
The largest size of a packet that can be sent over this interface in one go.
Physical and virtual interfaces (enp0s3, enp0s8, docker0) are set to 1500.
If a packet is larger than this then it is fragmented.
lo has an MTU of 65536. Since it's internal to the  CPU/RAM it can handle massive packets without fragmentation.

2.Type (Ethernet)
This identifies the hardware as Ethernet.

3. Flags 
UP: The interface is software-activated.
RUNNING: The interface is physically "plugged in".
BROADCAST: The interface can send a message to everyone on the local network.
MULTICAST: The interface can send data to a specific group of devices simultaneously.

4. TX/RX Statistics (Traffic Analysis)
RX / TX:
Packets: Total number of data chunks successfully moved.

Bytes: Total data volume.
Errors/Dropped: These should ideally be 0.
Dropped: Usually happens if the kernel's buffer is full.
Overruns: Indicates the CPU couldn't process the packets fast enough.

5. Scope 
Local and Global
Scopeid 0x20 <link>: This means the IPv6 address is only valid for the local "wire".
It cannot be used to talk to the internet.

Scopeid 0x0 <global>: These are addresses that (theoretically) can be reached from the outside world.

